			<item>
				<title>Minimizing Brainrot</title>
				<link>https://x3ero0.github.io/posts/minimizing-brainrot/</link>
				<pubDate>Tue, 17 Mar 2026 05:18:39 -0700</pubDate>
				<guid>https://x3ero0.github.io/posts/minimizing-brainrot/</guid>
				<description>&lt;p&gt;I recently came across &lt;span class=&#34;reference-cite&#34; id=&#34;cite_ref-1&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;reference-term&#34;&gt;PewDiePie&amp;rsquo;s video titled &amp;ldquo;A Secret that Fixes Your Brain&amp;rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;reference-marker&#34;&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;reference-link&#34; href=&#34;#cite_note-1&#34; aria-label=&#34;Jump to reference 1&#34;&gt;[1]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;. It&#xA;was a very entertaining video, as usual. In this video, he goes over how he&#xA;fixed YouTube by removing Shorts and recommendations from the algorithm. As a&#xA;long-time member of the 9-year-old army, I clicked on the video immediately.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But as I was watching it, I realized I was doing almost all of the things he&#xA;mentioned in his video about getting rid of brainrot. I was spending way too much time on&#xA;Instagram and Reddit, so a couple of weeks ago I decided that the simplest fix to&#xA;this problem was to remove as many triggers (anything that makes me want to go&#xA;back to these platforms) as possible.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
